{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CggB9eaLcd4s1zaWxxvTB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I searched the tools available in this session for anything that could query tissue expression data (GTEx, Human Protein Atlas, gene-expression lookups). The only connected server is Galaxy, which exposes workflow/history/tool-execution operations and a tool catalogue search — nothing that queries a tissue expression atlas, and there's no GTEx or HPA dataset loaded that I could compute against. So **this answer comes from my own knowledge, not from a lookup I performed in this session.**\n\nInsulin (gene *INS*, HGNC:6081, Ensembl ENSG00000254647, chr11p15.5) is expressed overwhelmingly in the **pancreas**, and within it specifically in the **beta cells of the islets of Langerhans**.\n\nTwo points worth keeping straight:\n\n- **In bulk-tissue atlases (GTEx, HPA consensus), pancreas is the top tissue by a very wide margin** — on the order of thousands of TPM, with every other tissue orders of magnitude lower. I'm recalling the magnitude, not reading it off a tool, so treat the exact TPM as approximate; if you need a citable number, GTEx v8/v10's *INS* gene page or HPA's consensus dataset is the place to pull it.\n- **Bulk pancreas actually understates it.** Islets are only ~1–2% of pancreatic mass, the rest being exocrine acinar tissue. In single-cell/islet data, *INS* is the single most abundant transcript in beta cells — it can account for a large fraction of a beta cell's mRNA pool. So the honest answer to \"which tissue\" is pancreas; the answer to \"which cell type\" is the pancreatic beta cell.\n\nMinor but real caveat: low-level *INS* transcription occurs in the **thymus** (AIRE-driven expression in medullary thymic epithelial cells, relevant to immune tolerance and type 1 diabetes risk via the *INS* VNTR), and trace signal is reported in a few other tissues. None of these approach pancreatic levels.\n\n## ANSWER\n\n**Pancreas** — specifically the beta cells of the islets of Langerhans, where *INS* is among the most abundant transcripts. Stated from domain knowledge; no expression-atlas tool was available in this session to verify the numeric value."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9299,"cache_read_input_tokens":20269,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9299},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cfbe87b9-4d9d-4d33-83b5-3d2cac50e974","uuid":"7f9f7777-435b-4416-bc44-31bdf599b64e","timestamp":"2026-11-03T14:46:25.121Z","request_id":"req_011CggB9eKhrwzMnFZdV366t"}
